 "statements": [
  {
   "id": "st_0001",
   "section_id": "sec_01",
   "text": "A pure quadratic is a quadratic function of the form f(x)=ax^2+bx+c with a≠0, whose graph is a parabola.",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "web",
     "url": "https://www.varsitytutors.com/practice/subjects/ap-physics-1/lessons/quadratic-models-and-optimization-max-min",
     "title": "Quadratic Models and Optimization (Max/Min) | Varsity Tutors"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "st_0002",
   "section_id": "sec_01",
   "text": "The function is a pure quadratic in two variables, x and y.",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0003",
     "start": 19.12,
     "end": 21.92
    },
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0004",
     "start": 22.4,
     "end": 27.2
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "st_0003",
   "section_id": "sec_01",
   "text": "Any pure quadratic in two variables can be written as x^T S x where S is a symmetric 2×2 matrix.",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0005",
     "start": 27.44,
     "end": 42.48
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "st_0004",
   "section_id": "sec_01",
   "text": "In this example, S is diagonal and its eigenvalues are 1 and b.",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0006",
     "start": 42.96,
     "end": 49.52
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "st_0005",
   "section_id": "sec_01",
   "text": "Here b represents the smaller eigenvalue of S.",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0007",
     "start": 49.68,
     "end": 52.72
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "st_0006",
   "section_id": "sec_01",
   "text": "For a symmetric positive matrix, the condition number is the ratio of its largest to smallest eigenvalue.",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "web",
     "url": "https://www.math.cmu.edu/~shlomo/VKI-Lectures/lecture1/node5.html",
     "title": "Condition Number of the Hessian"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "st_0007",
   "section_id": "sec_01",
   "text": "The condition number of S is λ_max/λ_min = 1/b.",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0008",
     "start": 53.28,
     "end": 70.08
    },
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0009",
     "start": 70.32,
     "end": 76.0
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "st_0008",
   "section_id": "sec_01",
   "text": "When b is very small, 1/b is large, indicating a high condition number and slow convergence.",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0010",
     "start": 76.4,
     "end": 84.72
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "st_0009",
   "section_id": "sec_01",
   "text": "An eigenvalue is the factor by which an eigenvector is stretched or compressed during a linear transformation.",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "web",
     "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eigenvalues_and_eigenvectors",
     "title": "Eigenvalues and eigenvectors - Wikipedia"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "st_0010",
   "section_id": "sec_01",
   "text": "Gradient descent is a first‑order iterative algorithm that uses the gradient to minimize a differentiable function.",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "web",
     "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gradient_descent",
     "title": "Gradient descent - Wikipedia"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "st_0011",
   "section_id": "sec_02",
   "text": "We can take explicit steps of the steepest descent algorithm.",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0015",
     "start": 105.28,
     "end": 114.72
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "st_0012",
   "section_id": "sec_02",
   "text": "At each iteration we can write down the update produced by steepest descent and monitor how fast it approaches the solution.",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0016",
     "start": 115.59,
     "end": 122.47
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "st_0013",
   "section_id": "sec_02",
   "text": "The example function is a bowl centered at the origin, so its minimum is at x* = (0,0).",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0019",
     "start": 128.47,
     "end": 131.59
    },
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0020",
     "start": 131.91,
     "end": 136.15
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "st_0014",
   "section_id": "sec_02",
   "text": "The key question is how many iterations are needed for steepest descent to reach the optimum.",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0022",
     "start": 143.03,
     "end": 146.55
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "st_0015",
   "section_id": "sec_02",
   "text": "Even though this is a simple, non‑typical example, it reveals the full behavior of the steepest descent method.",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0023",
     "start": 146.71,
     "end": 155.99
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "st_0016",
   "section_id": "sec_02",
   "text": "By running the algorithm we can view the exact sequence of points generated by steepest descent.",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0024",
     "start": 156.15,
     "end": 160.39
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "st_0017",
   "section_id": "sec_02",
   "text": "We can track how fast the iterates approach x* and consider strategies to accelerate convergence when it is sluggish.",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0025",
     "start": 160.55,
     "end": 177.83
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "st_0018",
   "section_id": "sec_02",
   "text": "Steepest descent is an iterative optimization method that moves from the current point in the direction of the negative gradient.",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "web",
     "url": "http://katselis.web.engr.illinois.edu/ECE586/Lecture3.pdf",
     "title": "Lecture 3: Steepest and Gradient Descent-Part I"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "st_0019",
   "section_id": "sec_03",
   "text": "The gradient of the linear function f(x,y)=2x+5y is the constant vector (2,5).",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0034",
     "start": 233.83,
     "end": 238.87
    },
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0035",
     "start": 239.46,
     "end": 250.34
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "st_0020",
   "section_id": "sec_03",
   "text": "Because the gradient is constant, it is the same at every point (x,y).",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0036",
     "start": 250.58,
     "end": 264.82
    },
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0058",
     "start": 347.46,
     "end": 354.58
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "st_0021",
   "section_id": "sec_03",
   "text": "The gradient direction indicates the steepest ascent; moving perpendicular to the level set increases the function fastest.",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0060",
     "start": 356.57,
     "end": 361.13
    },
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0061",
     "start": 361.29,
     "end": 368.41
    },
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0062",
     "start": 368.49,
     "end": 371.45
    },
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0063",
     "start": 371.69,
     "end": 373.29
    },
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0064",
     "start": 373.45,
     "end": 375.69
    },
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0072",
     "start": 401.37,
     "end": 408.97
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "st_0022",
   "section_id": "sec_03",
   "text": "For f=2x+5y, the level set where f=0 is the plane 2x+5y=0; staying in this plane keeps the function value constant.",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0068",
     "start": 387.37,
     "end": 394.57
    },
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0069",
     "start": 396.33,
     "end": 398.17
    },
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0072",
     "start": 401.37,
     "end": 408.97
    },
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0073",
     "start": 409.69,
     "end": 415.69
    },
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0078",
     "start": 439.05,
     "end": 440.65
    },
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0079",
     "start": 440.81,
     "end": 444.17
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "st_0023",
   "section_id": "sec_03",
   "text": "A level set of a function is the set of points where the function has a constant value; for two variables it is a level curve or contour line.",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "web",
     "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Level_set",
     "title": "Level set - Wikipedia"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "st_0024",
   "section_id": "sec_03",
   "text": "The Hessian matrix consists of second‑order partial derivatives; for this linear function all second derivatives are zero, so the Hessian is the zero 2×2 matrix.",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0036",
     "start": 250.58,
     "end": 264.82
    },
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0037",
     "start": 264.98,
     "end": 270.18
    },
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0039",
     "start": 275.22,
     "end": 276.82
    },
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0040",
     "start": 276.98,
     "end": 278.1
    },
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0041",
     "start": 278.26,
     "end": 278.98
    },
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0043",
     "start": 286.58,
     "end": 290.5
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "st_0025",
   "section_id": "sec_03",
   "text": "The Hessian matrix describes the local curvature of a function.",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "web",
     "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hessian_matrix",
     "title": "Hessian matrix - Wikipedia"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "st_0026",
   "section_id": "sec_03",
   "text": "The lecturer asks how the Hessian relates to the convexity of the function.",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0099",
     "start": 586.08,
     "end": 592.8
    },
    {
     "type": "lecture",
     "segment_id": "seg_0100",
     "start": 593.12,
     "end": 597.68
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "st_0027",
   "section_id": "sec_03",
   "text": "Geometrically, a function is convex if a line segment drawn from any point (x, f(x)) to another point (y, f(y)) lies above or on the graph of the function.",
   "sources": [
    {
     "type": "web",
     "url": "https://www.solver.com/convex-optimization",
     "title": "Optimization Problem Types - Convex Optimization | solver"
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
